What are Availability Zones?

Availability Zones are physically separate datacenters within the same Azure region.

Example:

Central India

      Region
         │
 ┌───────┼────────┐
 │       │        │
Zone 1 Zone 2 Zone 3

Each zone has:

Independent power
Independent cooling
Independent networking

If one zone fails, workloads in other zones can continue running.

Example

Deploy two virtual machines:

VM1 → Zone 1

VM2 → Zone 2

If Zone 1 experiences an outage, VM2 in Zone 2 remains available (assuming your application is designed for redundancy).

When Should You Use Availability Zones?

Use them for:

Production applications
Databases
AKS
Load Balancers
Critical virtual machines

They are generally not necessary for simple development or test environments.

What is a Resource Group?

A Resource Group (RG) is a logical container for Azure resources.

Resource Group

├── Virtual Machine
├── Virtual Network
├── Storage Account
├── AKS
├── Azure Container Registry
└── Key Vault

A resource belongs to one resource group.

Why Use Resource Groups?
Organize related resources
Apply RBAC
Manage costs
Apply tags
Delete an entire environment together
Real Enterprise Example
rg-product-dev

├── vnet-product-dev
├── aks-product-dev
├── acr-product-dev
├── kv-product-dev
├── log-product-dev
└── vm-github-runner

This mirrors the kind of environment you've been building.

Resource Group Naming Convention

A common pattern:

rg-<application>-<environment>

Examples:

rg-product-dev
rg-product-qa
rg-product-prod
rg-hr-prod
rg-payroll-dev
Best Practices
Separate Dev, QA, UAT, and Prod.
Use consistent naming conventions.
Tag resources (Environment, Owner, CostCenter, Project).
Avoid mixing unrelated applications in the same Resource Group.
Grant RBAC at the Resource Group level when appropriate.
